{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And when Abram heard that his brother was taken captive.</b> He clothed himself in might when he heard the matter, and immediately: ״וירק את חניכיו ילידי ביתו״ — he armed his trained men, those born in his household. And Scripture teaches you that his men, the members of his household who ate at his table, were three hundred and eighteen." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> Eighteen and three hundred — this is Eliezer, which amounts in gematria to 318 (שי״ח). And one should ask, for according to this midrash, three hundred and eighteen is not in its plain sense but rather a riddle about Eliezer alone, and we are not to remove the verse from its plain sense, since Scripture testifies concerning ילידי ביתו that they were 318 (שי״ח)." "<b>Rather,</b> the matter was thus: this entire number were the members of Abraham's household born in his home, who ate at his table; and once he roused himself to go out to war, he diminished them and emptied them out, for it is merit that brings victory, not a multitude of people. Therefore he commanded that all the sinners return, according to the Torah's law concerning those who go out to war, as Scripture says (Deuteronomy 20:8): ״מי האיש הירא וגו'״ — 'Who is the man who is afraid,' etc. And this is the sense of וירק, which includes both the sense of arming (זיין) and the sense of diminution, from the root רק. And once they were diminished, Abraham found himself with Eliezer alone. And even though it is written חניכיו (with the plural yod), the singular חניכו is included within the plural חניכיו — to tell you that all his trained men stood with his trained one, that is, with Eliezer alone. And what it says, ״אחיו״ — 'his brother' — ״והיה בן הרן אחיו״, and he was the son of Haran his brother — thereby informs us of the beauty of Abraham's character and his virtue, for he was zealous for the matter and turned to brotherhood and did not turn to the quarrel that had been between them, but roused himself in the matter as though he were truly his brother." "<b>And he pursued as far as Dan.</b> Until the attribute of Judgment fell upon them. And so Isaiah says (Isaiah 41:2): ״ירדפם יעבור שלום״, etc." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״וירדף עד דן״ — there his strength failed, because his descendants were destined to set up idolatry there, as it is written (I Kings 12:29): ״וישם את האחד בבית אל ואת האחד נתן בדן״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:27.553588330Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}